```
ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor
VOTE: red
MANDATORY_BASELINES:
  - {name: "SnapKV / H2O", why: "State-of-the-art attention-based KV-cache eviction/compression that already retains high-attention tokens across long contexts. The proposed method's use of 'attention/citation signal' directly competes with this.", repo_or_cite: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14469 (SnapKV) / https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14048 (H2O)"}
  - {name: "LLMLingua", why: "Standard baseline for extractive prompt compression in long-context scenarios.", repo_or_cite: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05736"}
STRAWMAN_RISKS:
  - Comparing an attention/salience-guided retention policy exclusively to naive recency-truncation (RT) is a structural strawman. RT is known to catastrophically fail on tasks requiring early-turn recall.
  - The L0 simulation is purely tautological: it manually injects a synthetic "salience" proxy into the data generation that dictates future references, and then "discovers" that exploiting this proxy yields high task success. This proves the code works, not the hypothesis.
MISSING_METRICS:
  - True predictive AUC of *real* LLM attention/citation signals for future references (synthetic salience bypasses the actual modeling problem).
  - End-to-end task completion rate on a real tool-use benchmark (e.g., τ-bench, SWE-bench), replacing the synthetic "referenced-span availability" proxy.
  - Computational overhead/latency of running the extraction policy versus native KV eviction.
KILLER_EXPERIMENT:
  - Run a real multi-step tool-use benchmark (e.g., τ-bench) with a real LLM. Compare the proposed extractive compressor directly against SnapKV and LLMLingua at strictly matched context/KV token budgets, measuring end-to-end task success and empirical attention-to-future-reference predictive AUC.
RATIONALE:
  - The evaluation is currently a circular synthetic exercise comparing against a toy baseline. You cannot claim an "attention/citation signal" compressor is effective without testing real attention on real tool outputs against the actual state-of-the-art in attention-based compression (SnapKV/H2O). The "make-or-break" negative (N3) is not refuted; it is merely hardcoded to succeed by the synthetic data generator.
```
